{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CfLfjhPff7gyQZsq5tPP8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is expected behavior, not a bug — confirmed in `src/docs/detecting-classes-in-source-files.mdx` (§\"Dynamic class names\", lines 65-69):\n\n> \"Since Tailwind scans your source files as plain text, it has no way of understanding string concatenation or interpolation in the programming language you're using.\"\n\nThe class detector (and the prettier plugin's sorter, which reuses this same extraction logic) treats each segment between `${...}` interpolations as an independent chunk of plain text — it can't parse across the interpolation boundary to know `py-1` and `px-1` belong to the same class list. This is why sorting only happens within each segment. The doc's recommended fix is to avoid constructing/splitting class strings dynamically — e.g. map complete class names via a lookup object instead of interpolating partial values into the middle of the string."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":492,"cache_read_input_tokens":17311,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":492},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"758a3f67-a914-4f60-8741-18ae24be2445","uuid":"10f7ec4a-ac24-4b6b-802e-f22068d58321","timestamp":"2026-09-23T16:14:44.248Z","request_id":"req_011CfLfjgx86eA1hkymcxnFF"}
